<section id="s-plateau" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Mismatch</div>
<h2>The Offline Supervised Fine-Tuning Plateau</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding accelerates Large Language Model (LLM) inference by pairing a large target model with a lightweight draft model. A mainstream way to build these draft models, such as <strong>EAGLE-3</strong> and <strong>DFlash</strong>, is offline Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) on trajectories generated by the target model. The drafter learns to predict the next token given a target-generated prefix.</p>

<p>However, offline SFT quickly plateaus. As shown in the training curve below, after an initial warm-up phase, continuing SFT on target trajectories yields no improvements in the draft model's acceptance length on test data. Even training offline on the prompt pool used for distillation can reduce the accepted length.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">plateau_curve.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Hover to inspect epochs</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="plateau-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The root cause is an <strong>offline-to-inference mismatch</strong> (or exposure bias). SFT trains the draft model exclusively on target-generated prefixes. But during speculative decoding at inference time, the draft model proposes token blocks under its own policy, creating draft-induced prefix states. When the drafter deviates from the target distribution, it encounters states it has never observed during offline SFT, leading to cascading verification failures and poor acceptance lengths.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-dilemma" data-code="dilemma">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Distillation Dilemma</div>
<h2>The OPD Dilemma for Speculative Drafters</h2>
<p>This exposure mismatch suggests using <strong>On-Policy Distillation (OPD)</strong>, where the target model supervises the student on states visited under the student's own policy. However, standard OPD is not directly applicable to draft models due to two critical issues:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Unstable Student Rollouts:</strong> Autoregressive draft modules (like EAGLE or DFlash) are trained to propose short token blocks under target model assistance rather than act as standalone generators. Forcing the draft model to generate entire sequences independently causes it to quickly produce degenerate, repetitive loops.</li>
  <li><strong>Target-Assisted Signal Loss:</strong> Utilizing the target model during rollout stabilizes generation because verification corrects incorrect tokens. But this correction forces the generation to follow the target model's distribution. The on-policy signal is lost because draft-model errors are discarded by verification, preventing the student from learning from its own mistakes.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">rollout_comparison.css</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Compare rollout behaviors</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="rollout-flex">
      <div class="rollout-box">
        <div class="rollout-title">Traditional OPD (Draft Self-Rollout) — Degenerate Loop</div>
        <div class="rollout-flow">
          <span class="token-badge correct">The</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">cat</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">sat</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">on</span>
          <span class="token-badge error loop">the</span>
          <span class="token-badge error loop">the</span>
          <span class="token-badge error loop">the</span>
          <span class="token-badge error loop">the</span>
          <span class="token-badge error loop">the</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rollout-box">
        <div class="rollout-title">Naive Target-Assisted Rollout — Losses On-Policy Signal</div>
        <div class="rollout-flow">
          <span class="token-badge correct">The</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">cat</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">sat <small style="color: var(--cyan)">(corrected from "say")</small></span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">on</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">the</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">mat <small style="color: var(--cyan)">(corrected from "moon")</small></span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">slowly</span>
          <span class="token-badge correct">tonight</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>To succeed, we need a method that maintains target-quality stable rollouts while preserving draft-induced error trajectories during verification. This is the core problem solved by <strong>Draft-OPD</strong>.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-mechanism" data-code="rollout">
<div class="section-no">03 — Anchored Rollout & Replay</div>
<h2>Draft-OPD: Anchored Rollout & Error-Position Replay</h2>
<p>Draft-OPD resolves this dilemma with a two-step generation and replay mechanism. First, it uses speculative decoding to generate stable target-quality sequences while recording the start of each draft block as an **anchor**. Next, it **replays** drafting from these anchors, forcing both the draft and target models to evaluate the proposed block — even rejected suffixes.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">draft_opd_steps.nav</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Step-by-step mechanism</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list">
      <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Step 1: Rollout with Speculative Decoding</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Generate a stable rollout via speculative decoding. Record anchor index <i>a<sub>m</sub></i> before proposing. Suppose draft proposes <code>["sat", "on", "the", "moon"]</code> from anchor 1.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Step 2: Verification and Anchor Recording</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Target model verifies draft block in parallel. It accepts <code>["sat", "on", "the"]</code>, rejects <code>"moon"</code>, and corrects it to <code>"mat"</code>. The next anchor index is recorded at the end of the accepted segment.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Step 3: Error Replay from Anchors</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Replay drafting from anchor <i>a<sub>m</sub></i>. Re-feed draft-proposed block <code>dm</code> (including rejected <code>"moon"</code>) to compute logits. The target model provides teacher distributions on these exact states.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Step 4: Distillation Update</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Partition proposed tokens into accepted and rejected categories. Apply asymmetric KL losses on the computed logits to optimize the draft model's parameters.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>‹ Previous</button>
