      <span id="step-prog" style="font-family: var(--ff-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--muted); margin-left: auto;">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>By replaying from the anchors, Draft-OPD evaluates both accepted proposals and rejected proposals on the target model. This allows the draft model to receive informative error signals from the target model on its own deviations without compromising sequence stability.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-asymmetric" data-code="loss">
<div class="section-no">04 — Acceptance-Aware Loss</div>
<h2>Acceptance-Aware Asymmetric KL Loss</h2>
<p>Once draft proposals are collected, they are partitioned into <strong>accepted tokens</strong> (I<sub>acc</sub>) and <strong>rejected tokens</strong> (I<sub>rej</sub>). Rather than applying a single divergence to all tokens, Draft-OPD applies different KL directions to match their asymmetric roles:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Forward KL for Accepted Tokens:</strong> Encourages the draft model to cover the target distribution's modes at states where they already align. This reinforces correct predictions on target paths.</li>
  <li><strong>Reverse KL for Rejected Tokens:</strong> Penalizes the draft model's high-probability incorrect modes when the target model disagrees. This actively suppresses the draft's error modes on draft-induced states.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout green">
  <div class="co-t">Intuition — Why Different KL Directions?</div>
  <p>Imagine the target model assigns probabilities to the next token in a sentence about European capitals:</p>
  <table class="results-table" style="margin:.65rem 0 .5rem">
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Target P</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>"Paris"</td><td class="mn">0.55</td><td>strong correct answer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Lyon"</td><td class="mn">0.30</td><td>also fine</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Berlin"</td><td class="mn">0.10</td><td>weak but plausible</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Banana"</td><td class="mn">0.05</td><td>almost certainly wrong</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Forward KL &mdash; D<sub>KL</sub>(P&thinsp;‖&thinsp;Q), used on accepted tokens:</strong> the loss is <em>weighted by the target P</em>. If the draft assigns near-zero to "Paris" (P=0.55), the penalty blows up toward &infin;. The gradient says <em>"cover every mode the target has mass on"</em> &mdash; a <strong>mean-seeking</strong> pull. On accepted tokens, where the target model already agrees with the draft's context, this reinforces the correct modes without letting the draft miss any of them.</p>
  <p><strong>Reverse KL &mdash; D<sub>KL</sub>(Q&thinsp;‖&thinsp;P), used on rejected tokens:</strong> the loss is <em>weighted by the draft Q</em>. If the draft confidently predicts "Banana" (Q=0.25) but the target strongly disagrees (P=0.05), it pays a large penalty; but if the draft simply ignores "Berlin" altogether, the cost is zero. The gradient says <em>"don't bet anywhere the target says no"</em> &mdash; a <strong>mode-seeking / zero-forcing</strong> push. On rejected tokens, where the draft has already gone off-track, this directly crushes the overconfident incorrect modes.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>The asymmetry is load-bearing:</strong> accepted tokens need <em>coverage</em> (don't miss the target's modes &rarr; forward KL pulls Q up); rejected tokens need <em>suppression</em> (don't overestimate where the target disagrees &rarr; reverse KL pushes Q down). Applying a single symmetric loss applies the wrong pressure in one of the two regions.</p>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">asymmetric_kl.inspector</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Toggle tokens to see KL behavior</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="viz-controls">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="btn-acc">Accepted Token ("sat")</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="btn-rej">Rejected Token ("moon")</button>
    </div>
