    <div class="viz-caption" id="kl-caption">
      Loading caption...
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This asymmetric loss formulation directly targets the draft model's finite capacity: forward KL aligns the base distribution, while reverse KL acts as a zero-forcing penalty that cleanses draft-induced errors.</p>

<p>The empirical advantage of reverse KL on rejected tokens is not accidental — it falls directly from how rejection is defined. A token is rejected when q(x) &gt; p(x): the draft overestimates it relative to the target. Reverse KL = &Sigma; Q(x) log(Q(x)/P(x)) is weighted by Q, so it registers a large signal exactly where Q is large and P is small — which is the rejected-token condition by construction. Forward KL = &Sigma; P(x) log(P(x)/Q(x)) is weighted by P, which is <em>small</em> on rejected tokens; those terms barely contribute even though the draft's error there is large.</p>

<table class="results-table" style="margin:1rem 0">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Loss on rejected tokens</th><th>Weighted by</th><th>Penalizes</th><th>Fixes q &gt; p?</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Forward KL &mdash; D<sub>KL</sub>(P&thinsp;‖&thinsp;Q)</td>
      <td class="mn">P</td>
      <td>Q missing where P has mass</td>
      <td>✗ &mdash; P is small here; barely contributes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row">
      <td>Reverse KL &mdash; D<sub>KL</sub>(Q&thinsp;‖&thinsp;P)</td>
      <td class="mn">Q</td>
      <td>Q having mass where P doesn't</td>
      <td>✓ &mdash; Q is large here; dominates the sum</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout amber">
  <div class="co-t">What the Empirical Win Tells You</div>
  <p style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>The dominant draft failure mode is false positives, not false negatives.</strong> If the draft's main error were <em>missing</em> correct tokens (q &lt; p — under-confident), forward KL would win, since it pulls Q up toward P's uncovered modes. The fact that reverse KL wins says the actual failure is the draft <em>overassigning probability</em> to tokens the target dislikes (q &gt; p — overconfident). In on-policy rollouts the draft operates in draft-induced states — prefixes it generated itself, which are subtly off-distribution. In those states its learned shortcuts fire on tokens that look right given its own context but that the target disagrees with. The error is excess confidence.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>Conservative Q maximizes acceptance; reverse KL produces conservative Q.</strong> Expected accepted length scales with &prod; min(1, p(x<sub>t</sub>) / q(x<sub>t</sub>)). This product is maximized when q(x) &le; p(x) everywhere — a draft that is systematically slightly underconfident beats an overconfident one. Reverse KL's mode-seeking behavior concentrates Q inside P's footprint and refuses to assign mass outside it, naturally producing this conservative regime. Forward KL's mean-seeking behavior inflates Q on P's low-probability tail, worsening the acceptance ratio at exactly those positions.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>The acceptance criterion is itself asymmetric in the same direction.</strong> Speculative decoding only rejects when q &gt; p; it never rejects when q &lt; p (under-proposals are accepted with probability 1). The training loss should be asymmetric in the same direction — hard zero-forcing penalty for q &gt; p (reverse KL on rejected tokens), soft coverage pull for q &lt; p (forward KL on accepted tokens). The empirical result confirms the loss was set up with the right handedness.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-decay" data-code="decay">
<div class="section-no">05 — Exponential Position Decay</div>
<h2>Exponential Position Decay</h2>
<p>In speculative decoding, draft tokens are verified sequentially. If a token fails verification, the remaining tokens in the proposed block are discarded, regardless of whether they match the target distribution. Thus, errors at the beginning of a block have a much larger impact on average accepted length than errors near the end.</p>

<p>Draft-OPD reflects this sequential priority by applying an exponentially decaying weight to rejected tokens based on their position $k$ in the draft block:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Rejected Token Position Weight</div>
  w_k = &gamma;<sup>k - 1</sup> <span class="math-comment"># &gamma; is the decay factor (typically 0.8)</span>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
